{# ── Chapter: Hook ── #}
<section class="case-chapter" data-chapter="hook">
  <h2>The theme toggle that re-rendered every time a notification arrived</h2>
  <div class="tone-junior">
    <p>
      An app has one <code>AppContext</code> providing everything global: the logged-in <code>user</code>, the current <code>theme</code>, and a live <code>notifications</code> array that grows every time a new notification arrives over a websocket. A small header component reads only <code>theme</code> from that context to decide which icon to show.
    </p>
    <p>
      With React DevTools' render highlighting on, that theme icon lights up on every single incoming notification, dozens of times a minute during busy periods, even though its own props and the specific value it reads, <code>theme</code>, never change. The component is doing real re-render work for data it doesn't use and never displays.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-mid">
    <p>
      This isn't a bug in React, it's exactly how <code>useContext</code> is specified to behave: every component that calls <code>useContext(AppContext)</code> re-renders whenever the Provider's <code>value</code> changes, full stop, regardless of which fields inside that value the component actually reads. React has no built-in way to subscribe to "just the <code>theme</code> field" of a context, the subscription granularity is the whole context, not any part of it.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-staff">
    <p>
      This scales badly in a specific, predictable direction: the more unrelated concerns get bundled into one context "for convenience," the more of the app re-renders on every unrelated update to any one of them. A notifications feed updating multiple times a second turns every context consumer in the tree, no matter how unrelated its actual concern, into a component re-rendering multiple times a second, unless something explicitly stops it (<code>React.memo</code> alone won't, since the component still re-renders when its context value's identity changes, only its own re-render from a parent prop change would be skipped).
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

{# ── Chapter: Concept ── #}
<section class="case-chapter" data-chapter="concept">
  <h2>Split the context by what actually changes together, then memoize each value</h2>
  <div class="tone-junior">
    <p>
      The fix is to stop putting unrelated data in one context. Give <code>user</code>, <code>theme</code>, and <code>notifications</code> each their own context, so a component that only calls <code>useContext(ThemeContext)</code> only re-renders when theme-related state actually changes, not when notifications does.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-mid">
    <p>
      There's a second, easy-to-miss half of this fix: even a single-purpose context still needs its <code>value</code> to be memoized. If a provider does <code>value=&#123;&#123; theme, setTheme &#125;&#125;</code> inline, that object is a brand-new reference on every render of the provider, and every consumer re-renders on every provider render regardless of whether <code>theme</code> itself changed, because React compares context value by reference, not by deep equality.
    </p>
    <pre style="overflow-x:auto;background:var(--casebook-surface-2);padding:12px;border-radius:8px;font-size:0.8125rem;"><code>// Broken: one context, unmemoized value
const AppContext = createContext();
function AppProvider({ children }) {
  const [user, setUser] = useState(null);
  const [theme, setTheme] = useState('light');
  const [notifications, setNotifications] = useState([]);
  const value = { user, setUser, theme, setTheme, notifications, setNotifications };
  return &lt;AppContext.Provider value={value}&gt;{children}&lt;/AppContext.Provider&gt;;
  // every consumer re-renders on ANY state change above, AND on every
  // AppProvider render even if nothing changed, since `value` is a new
  // object literal every time
}

// Fixed: split contexts, each value memoized independently
const ThemeContext = createContext();
function ThemeProvider({ children }) {
  const [theme, setTheme] = useState('light');
  const value = useMemo(() =&gt; ({ theme, setTheme }), [theme]);
  return &lt;ThemeContext.Provider value={value}&gt;{children}&lt;/ThemeContext.Provider&gt;;
}
// NotificationsProvider and UserProvider follow the same shape, each with
// their own useMemo'd value — a component calling useContext(ThemeContext)
// now only re-renders when `theme` itself actually changes.</code></pre>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-staff">
    <p>
      Splitting contexts scopes re-renders by concern, but within one context, every consumer still re-renders together whenever that context's value changes, splitting <code>notifications</code> into its own context doesn't help a component that reads only the notification <em>count</em> avoid re-rendering on every individual notification's content change. For that finer granularity, either derive and memoize a narrower value close to where it's read, or reach for a selector-based library (<code>use-context-selector</code>, or state libraries built around fine-grained subscriptions like Zustand or Jotai) that subscribes components to a slice of state rather than a whole context value.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

{# ── Chapter: Story ── #}
<section class="case-chapter" data-chapter="story-1">
  <h2>The dashboard that got measurably faster from three fewer lines of coupling</h2>
  <div class="tone-junior">
    <p>
      A team profiled a dashboard that felt sluggish during live data updates and found, via React DevTools' Profiler, that a sidebar's user-avatar component was re-rendering on every one of dozens of per-second data-grid updates, despite the avatar's own props never changing. Tracing it back, both the avatar and the data grid read from the same single <code>DashboardContext</code>, and the grid's frequent updates were the ones actually changing the shared context value.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-mid">
    <p>
      Splitting <code>DashboardContext</code> into a <code>UserContext</code> (rarely changes) and a <code>LiveDataContext</code> (changes constantly) immediately removed the avatar, and every other user-info consumer, from the Profiler's flame graph during data updates, they simply stopped subscribing to the thing that was actually changing.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-staff">
    <p>
      The team also found, while doing this, a provider that memoized its context value correctly but recreated the setter functions inside it on every render (<code>value=&#123;&#123; theme, setTheme: (t) =&gt; setThemeState(t) &#125;&#125;</code> instead of a stable, top-level <code>setTheme</code>), which defeated the <code>useMemo</code> entirely since the object's contents, and therefore whether the memo's dependency array should be considered "changed," included a function recreated fresh every render. The general lesson: memoizing the context value object is necessary but not sufficient, everything inside that object needs a stable identity too, or the memoization is cosmetic.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

