{# ── Chapter: FE Depth ── #}
<section class="case-chapter" data-chapter="fe-depth">
  <h2>Memoize all the way down, and know when splitting still isn't enough</h2>
  <div class="tone-junior">
    <p>
      A context split with an unmemoized value object still re-renders every consumer on every render of the provider itself, not just on real data changes, because the object literal is a new reference each time. Always wrap a context's <code>value</code> in <code>useMemo</code> with the actual dependencies listed, not an empty array and not the whole state object by habit.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-mid">
    <p>
      Setter functions from <code>useState</code> are already stable across renders, use them directly in the memoized value rather than wrapping them in a new inline arrow function, an inline wrapper is a fresh function reference every render and defeats the memo the same way an unmemoized object would.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-staff">
    <p>
      Splitting contexts and memoizing values solves re-renders caused by <em>unrelated</em> data sharing one context, it does not solve a consumer that reads a large, single, frequently-changing value (a big <code>notifications</code> array) and only cares about a derived slice of it (the count). For that, memoize the derived value at the point of use (<code>useMemo(() =&gt; notifications.length, [notifications])</code> still re-renders when the array reference changes, so this specific case usually wants the count tracked as its own piece of state, or a selector-based state library that can subscribe to a computed slice without subscribing to the whole array's identity.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

